<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Debre Berhan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Debre Berhan is a town in the highlands of North Shewa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a>. It was founded by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> in the 1450s after a miraculous light was said to have appeared there, and served as his capital. In the 19th century it was again a royal residence of the kings of Shewa.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/debre-berhan.jpg" alt="Debre Berhan" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Debre_Birham_(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Debre Berhan lies in the Semien Shewa Zone of the Amhara Region, about 130 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addis-ababa">Addis Ababa</a> on Ethiopian Highway 2. At 2,840 metres above sea level it is the highest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. It has a subtropical highland climate and is one of the coolest towns in the subtropical zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The historian David Buxton thought the town was bound to regain importance because it commands an important focus of routes, and the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> passes through its easy gap, avoiding impassable gorges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> founded the town after a light seen in the sky, which he took as a sign of God's approval for the stoning of a group of heretics 38 days earlier. He ordered a church built, then an extensive palace and a second church dedicated to Saint Cyriacus, and spent 12 of the last 14 years of his life there. The historian Richard Pankhurst dates the church to 1456 and argues that the light was Halley's Comet. His son Baeda Maryam spent the first part of his reign there but then returned to the itinerant court, a camp that moved constantly because the needs of the court and army for firewood and food were so heavy.</p><p>In the 16th century Emperor Lebna Dengel mustered his troops there against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-ibrahim-al-ghazi">Ahmad ibn Ibrahim</a>, who after the Battle of Amba Sel mustered his own army there twice, in 1535 for campaigns against Tigray, Begemder and Gojjam. He ordered the buildings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> destroyed. The town regained importance under Asfaw Wossen of Shewa (1775-1808), who built a palace there. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahle-selassie">Sahle Selassie</a> rebuilt it after it was ravaged by Abichu Oromo and built the church of the Selassie. The treaty of Wadara of 1878 required that the capital of Shewa be moved there from Liche. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a> rebuilt the church in 1906.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town is the administrative centre of the North Shewa Zone and was home to a population of 65,231 at the 2007 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, of whom 94.12 percent reported Ethiopian Orthodox Christianity. Most people speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> as a first language. It has a long tradition of rug making and was the site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s first wool factory, which began production on 1 January 1965. Debre Berhan University, one of thirteen founded in 2007, is in the town. None of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a>'s buildings survives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Debre Birhan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successors, at first with the title meridazmach, gradually extended their rule over the surrounding Oromo and Amhara districts, with capitals at Ankober and later Angolalla and <b>Debre Berhan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shewa">Kingdom of Shewa</a></li></ul></section>
